  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Precision Steering Curve Refinement\n\n## Context\n\nWe have restored an earlier working version of STARFALL.\n\nIts mouse input and reticle behavior work correctly. However, the flight feel needs improvement.\n\nThe user identified two specific problems:\n\n1. Small mouse movements near the center produce excessive rotation, making precise aiming difficult.\n2. When the reticle first reaches the aim boundary, steering strength increases abruptly.\n\n## Objective\n\nImprove the existing steering response while preserving the current working input and reticle architecture.\n\nTarget style: Traditional Arcade Space Combat.\n\n## Step 1 \342\200\224 Inspect the Existing Implementation\n\nTrace the current pipeline:\n\n- Pointer Lock relative mouse input.\n- Reticle position calculation.\n- Aim boundary normalization.\n- Mouse displacement to steering demand.\n- Yaw and pitch calculations.\n- Existing acceleration and damping.\n\nIdentify the exact function that maps reticle position to steering strength.\n\nDetermine whether the abrupt boundary response comes from:\n\n- A piecewise response curve.\n- A deadzone transition.\n- A boundary clamp.\n- A sudden multiplier change.\n- A virtual joystick activation threshold.\n- Conflicting steering calculations.\n\n## Step 2 \342\200\224 Refine the Existing Response Curve\n\nPreserve the existing architecture.\n\nDesired behavior:\n\n- Very low sensitivity near the center.\n- Gradually increasing steering strength toward the boundary.\n- No abrupt acceleration when the reticle first reaches the boundary.\n- Predictable response outside the boundary, if the current design supports it.\n- Strong enough maximum steering for fast directional changes.\n\nUse a continuous, monotonic response curve.\n\nAvoid adding unnecessary smoothing, artificial inertia, or a new virtual joystick system.\n\n## Step 3 \342\200\224 Strict Preservation Requirements\n\nDo not modify:\n\n- Pointer Lock implementation.\n- `movementX` / `movementY` input handling.\n- Working physical mouse-edge continuity behavior.\n- Reticle recentering behavior.\n- Aim boundary dimensions or shape.\n- Flight HUD.\n- Camera controller.\n- Combat, weapons, bots, or missions.\n\nDo not replace the existing Flight Controller.\n\nDo not introduce a second competing steering pipeline.\n\n## Step 4 \342\200\224 Controlled Calibration\n\nFirst establish the current response at:\n\n- Center.\n- 25% of the aim boundary.\n- 50% of the aim boundary.\n- 75% of the aim boundary.\n- 100% of the aim boundary.\n\nCompare the current response with the proposed response.\n\nModify only the existing response mapping.\n\nDo not change maximum turn rates and overall sensitivity in the same pass.\n\n## Acceptance Tests\n\n1. Small center movements allow precise aiming.\n2. Slow continuous tracking remains predictable.\n3. Crossing the aim boundary does not cause a sudden turn-rate jump.\n4. Larger movements progressively produce stronger turning.\n5. Large directional changes remain practical.\n6. Pointer Lock and reticle behavior remain unchanged.\n7. Test in an actual browser with a real mouse.\n\nReport the exact source changes and actual browser test results.\n\nIf real browser testing is unavailable, explicitly state that the implementation remains unverified. Do not claim successful gameplay validation based only on mathematical simulations."
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Precision Steering Curve Refinement\n\n## Context\n\nWe have restored an earlier working version of STARFALL.\n\nIts mouse input and reticle behavior work correctly. However, the flight feel needs improvement.\n\nThe user identified two specific problems:\n\n1. Small mouse movements near the center produce excessive rotation, making precise aiming difficult.\n2. When the reticle first reaches the aim boundary, steering strength increases abruptly.\n\n## Objective\n\nImprove the existing steering response while preserving the current working input and reticle architecture.\n\nTarget style: Traditional Arcade Space Combat.\n\n## Step 1 \342\200\224 Inspect the Existing Implementation\n\nTrace the current pipeline:\n\n- Pointer Lock relative mouse input.\n- Reticle position calculation.\n- Aim boundary normalization.\n- Mouse displacement to steering demand.\n- Yaw and pitch calculations.\n- Existing acceleration and damping.\n\nIdentify the exact function that maps reticle position to steering strength.\n\nDetermine whether the abrupt boundary response comes from:\n\n- A piecewise response curve.\n- A deadzone transition.\n- A boundary clamp.\n- A sudden multiplier change.\n- A virtual joystick activation threshold.\n- Conflicting steering calculations.\n\n## Step 2 \342\200\224 Refine the Existing Response Curve\n\nPreserve the existing architecture.\n\nDesired behavior:\n\n- Very low sensitivity near the center.\n- Gradually increasing steering strength toward the boundary.\n- No abrupt acceleration when the reticle first reaches the boundary.\n- Predictable response outside the boundary, if the current design supports it.\n- Strong enough maximum steering for fast directional changes.\n\nUse a continuous, monotonic response curve.\n\nAvoid adding unnecessary smoothing, artificial inertia, or a new virtual joystick system.\n\n## Step 3 \342\200\224 Strict Preservation Requirements\n\nDo not modify:\n\n- Pointer Lock implementation.\n- `movementX` / `movementY` input handling.\n- Working physical mouse-edge continuity behavior.\n- Reticle recentering behavior.\n- Aim boundary dimensions or shape.\n- Flight HUD.\n- Camera controller.\n- Combat, weapons, bots, or missions.\n\nDo not replace the existing Flight Controller.\n\nDo not introduce a second competing steering pipeline.\n\n## Step 4 \342\200\224 Controlled Calibration\n\nFirst establish the current response at:\n\n- Center.\n- 25% of the aim boundary.\n- 50% of the aim boundary.\n- 75% of the aim boundary.\n- 100% of the aim boundary.\n\nCompare the current response with the proposed response.\n\nModify only the existing response mapping.\n\nDo not change maximum turn rates and overall sensitivity in the same pass.\n\n## Acceptance Tests\n\n1. Small center movements allow precise aiming.\n2. Slow continuous tracking remains predictable.\n3. Crossing the aim boundary does not cause a sudden turn-rate jump.\n4. Larger movements progressively produce stronger turning.\n5. Large directional changes remain practical.\n6. Pointer Lock and reticle behavior remain unchanged.\n7. Test in an actual browser with a real mouse.\n\nReport the exact source changes and actual browser test results.\n\nIf real browser testing is unavailable, explicitly state that the implementation remains unverified. Do not claim successful gameplay validation based only on mathematical simulations."
