    thinking: "**Analyzing the Pipeline\'s Math**\n\nI\'m diving deep into the mathematical underpinnings of the pipeline, tracing each step in the implementation. It\'s becoming clearer with each line of code. Currently, I am analyzing the pointer lock section.\n\n\n**Examining Mouse Input**\n\nI\'ve been looking closely at how relative mouse input is handled. Specifically, I\'m examining the `handleMouseMove` function and how it accumulates movement data. I am further analyzing the filtering, and how the `consumeSnapshot` function provides delta values and pointer lock state.\n\n\n**Evaluating Steering Formula**\n\nI\'m now focused on the steering demand calculations within the `AimBoundary` class. I\'ve broken down the `evaluateVirtualJoystick` function, noting the radius clamping, and the call to `evaluateResponseCurveScalar`. I\'m going through the math to understand the precise formula and its derivatives. I\'m paying close attention to the `STEERING_CONFIG` defaults and their impact on the output.\n\n\n**Calculating Response Curve**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the response curve\'s behavior and sensitivity. I\'ve begun computing specific points based on the parameters: deadzone (`dz = 0.06`), precision sensitivity (`k = 0.85`), and curve exponent (`p = 1.35`). Specifically, I calculated the output at 25% deflection (r=0.25). The results show a significant output percentage even at relatively small deflections. I\'m now going to analyze at r = 0.10 and r = 0.15, as well as the slope `d(shaped)/dr` to fully assess the curve\'s impact.\n\n\n**Investigating Sensitivity Issues**\n\nI\'m now investigating a user report regarding excessive rotation with small mouse movements. I\'ve re-examined the code, focusing on how the response curve and steering demand are calculated and applied together. I\'m double-checking the interplay between `evaluateResponseCurveScalar` and other affected code segments and looking for the cause of the issue.\n\n\n"
