    thinking: "**Examining Crucial Code**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the code, meticulously examining `evaluateResponseCurveScalar`, `STEERING_CONFIG`, `SettingsPanel.tsx`, and `FlightController.ts`. Specifically, I\'m focusing on the implementation of `evaluateResponseCurveScalar` within `src/engine/AimBoundary.ts` (lines 285-3).\n\n\n**Analyzing Steering Mechanics**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the `evaluateResponseCurveScalar` function and the `STEERING_CONFIG` values within the code. I\'m focusing on their direct influence on steering sensitivity, particularly near the deadzone, to quantitatively assess the first user-identified issue. The geometric implications are becoming clearer as I trace how these values shape the response curve and address the second user-identified problem. I\'m now looking at how these parameters influence both problems together.\n\n\n**Quantifying Steepness Analysis**\n\nI\'ve quantified the steepness of the response curve near the deadzone. Calculations confirm a very rapid increase in rotation, especially given the `yawSpeed = 2.1 rad/s`, which corresponds to `0.21 rad/s` just 25% off-center.\n\n\n**Investigating Response Jumps**\n\nI\'m now investigating the potential for sudden steering increases near the aim boundary. Specifically, I\'m meticulously analyzing `evaluateResponseCurveScalar` for any jumps or steep slopes as the reticle approaches `r = 1.0`, calculating the derivative of the function to assess its behavior. I\'m also systematically checking each of the six identified potential causes for the issue, with the aim of pinpointing the exact mechanism responsible for the problematic steering behavior.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Clamping Effects**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how `clampToRoundedRect` impacts the joystick coordinates, particularly near the aim boundary edges. I\'m examining the clamping behavior when a candidate point falls outside a straight edge. The interactions between `clampToRoundedRect`, `computeRoundedRectRayRadius`, and `evaluateVirtualJoystick` in `FlightController.ts` are becoming clearer. The analysis of `FlightController.ts` lines 367-377 reveals how `clampToRoundedRect` modifies joystick values, specifically how clamped coordinates are determined at the straight edges of the boundary.\n\n\n**Exploring Parameter Dependencies**\n\nI\'m now investigating the relationship between `r`, `u`, and the boundary. I\'m focusing on scenarios with `r` values of 0.75, 0.9, and 1.0, and how these values change when moving along the boundary or diagonally. I\'m also planning to check `settings.responseCurve` (within `loadSavedSettings` or `STEERING_CONFIG`) and `stepAngularVelocity`, as well as `FlightHUD.tsx`. Specifically, I\'m now looking at `stepAngularVelocity` in `FlightController.ts` lines 155-179, to see how it affects the angular velocity calculation.\n\n\n**Evaluating Acceleration Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now investigating the acceleration mechanics in `FlightController.ts` lines 426-428. Initial calculations show smooth acceleration, not an abrupt jump. I\'m focusing on the `dampingRate`, `maxAngularAccel`, and the influence of `dt` at 60 FPS and calculating `maxStep`. I\'m comparing this to the user\'s reported experience, and how they relate to the user-reported issues related to excessive rotation with small movements and the sudden steering increase at the boundary, which I want to evaluate with the curve `f(r)` in `evaluateResponseCurveScalar`.\n\n\n**Investigating Response Discontinuities**\n\nI\'m now focusing on checking if any discontinuity exists in either `evaluateResponseCurveScalar`, `computeRoundedRectRayRadius`, or any other related function. I\'m planning a small node script to comprehensively check the output across angles and radii for both `evaluateResponseCurveScalar` and `evaluateVirtualJoystick`. I\'ll also investigate `localStorage` and other possible external factors.\n\n\n**Analyzing Boundary Calculations**\n\nI\'m now focusing on `computeRoundedRectRayRadius`, specifically lines 248-275 in `AimBoundary.ts`. I\'ve examined how it computes the boundary radius for horizontal, vertical, and diagonal directions. I need to understand why line 266 is crucial, because it relates to straight edge hits. I\'m carefully analyzing its conditions, like `hitX <= innerHalfW + 1e-6 || hitY <= innerHalfH + 1e-6`, to see how those interactions between straight edges and coordinates work. I\'m focusing on those conditions, specifically when either coordinate falls on a straight edge.\n\n\n**Verifying Geometric Precision**\n\nI\'ve been working to understand the boundary calculations, and I\'m now certain the logic is geometrically sound. I\'m satisfied that the conditions within `computeRoundedRectRayRadius` accurately handle corner and straight-edge hits. Now, I will meticulously analyze `evaluateResponseCurveScalar`, including how the scalar changes the angular velocity. I am planning to inspect the derivative of the function.\n\n\n"
