  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing smPow(1.35)**\n\nOkay, I\'ve started digging into `smPow(1.35)` now, particularly comparing its behavior to `OLD`. At the center (r=0), they appear identical, both yielding 0.0000. I also observed that `smPow(1.35)` exhibits a slope of 0.00 here.\n\n\n**Evaluating Comparisons**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a direct comparison between the old method and smPow(1.35) at different aim boundary percentages. Examining r=0.15, I found smPow offers seven times lower output and 3.6x less slope compared to OLD. At r=0.25, it\'s nearly three times lower, which seems ideal for fine tracking. The mid-range shows a very smooth and predictable behavior. However, at r=0.75, I will re-examine whether a peak slope of 1.74 feels super smooth compared to previous readings.\n\n\n**Refining Parameter Interactions**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into how `k` and `p` interact within `evaluateResponseCurveScalar`, specifically targeting a gentler mid-range slope (around 1.55 instead of 1.75). I need to ensure C1 continuity at `r = dz` and `r = 1.0` while experimenting with incorporating `k` in various ways. I\'m also exploring `u = (r - dz) / (1 - dz)`, `t = Math.pow(u, p)`, `smooth = t * t * (3 - 2 * t)` and how `precisionSensitivity` (`k in [0.4, 1.2]`) can influence it, without introducing any breaks in the curve. Finally, I\'ll incorporate and test my understanding of the old code by modifying it.\n\n\n**Investigating Function Failures**\n\nI\'ve realized the previous code\'s issues stemmed from the rapid initial growth of `u^p` (with p=1.35) combined with scaling by `k=0.85` and the lack of tapering at the boundaries. I\'m now exploring a refined approach using `g(u) = s * (k + (1 - k) * s)`, where `s` is a smoothstep function of `u^p`. I\'m evaluating its properties to ensure it meets the necessary conditions at u = 0 and u = 1, ensuring continuity.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "lTz_aue3Csjpz7IP7KigoA8"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_11423922079374423194"
      name: "run_command"
      arguments_json: "{\"CommandLine\":\"node -e \'\\nconst dz = 0.06;\\nfunction evalCurve(fn, r) {\\n  const eps = 0.001;\\n  const v = fn(r);\\n  const dv = (fn(Math.min(1, r+eps)) - fn(Math.max(0, r-eps))) / (Math.min(1, r+eps) - Math.max(0, r-eps));\\n  return { v, dv };\\n}\\nfunction oldCurve(r) {\\n  if (r <= dz) return 0; if (r >= 1) return 1;\\n  const u = (r - dz) / (1 - dz);\\n  const s = u*u*(3-2*u);\\n  return Math.pow(u, 1.35) * (0.85 + 0.15 * s);\\n}\\nfunction newCurve(r, p=1.35, k=0.85) {\\n  if (r <= dz) return 0; if (r >= 1) return 1;\\n  const u = (r - dz) / (1 - dz);\\n  const t = Math.pow(u, p);\\n  const s = t * t * (3 - 2 * t);\\n  return s * (k + (1 - k) * s);\\n}\\nconst pts = [0, 0.06, 0.15, 0.25, 0.35, 0.50, 0.65, 0.75, 0.85, 0.95, 1.00];\\nconsole.log(\\\"r    | OLD (1.35,0.85)  | NEW(p=1.20,k=0.85) | NEW(p=1.35,k=0.85) | NEW(p=1.00,k=0.85) | NEW(p=1.25,k=0.80)\\\");\\nfor (const r of pts) {\\n  const a = evalCurve(oldCurve, r);\\n  const b = evalCurve((x)=>newCurve(x, 1.20, 0.85), r);\\n  const c = evalCurve((x)=>newCurve(x, 1.35, 0.85), r);\\n  const d = evalCurve((x)=>newCurve(x, 1.00, 0.85), r);\\n  const e = evalCurve((x)=>newCurve(x, 1.25, 0.80), r);\\n  console.log(\\n    r.toFixed(2).padEnd(5) + \\\"| \\\" +\\n    `${a.v.toFixed(4)} (${a.dv.toFixed(2)})`.padEnd(17) + \\\"| \\\" +\\n    `${b.v.toFixed(4)} (${b.dv.toFixed(2)})`.padEnd(19) + \\\"| \\\" +\\n    `${c.v.toFixed(4)} (${c.dv.toFixed(2)})`.padEnd(19) + \\\"| \\\" +\\n    `${d.v.toFixed(4)} (${d.dv.toFixed(2)})`.padEnd(19) + \\\"| \\\" +\\n    `${e.v.toFixed(4)} (${e.dv.toFixed(2)})`\\n  );\\n}\\n\'\", \"Cwd\":\"/app/applet\", \"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":5000, \"toolAction\":\"Testing refined steering curves\", \"toolSummary\":\"Test refined steering curves\"}"
