    thinking: "**Observing Power Function Behavior**\n\nI\'m struck by the consistency between `smPow(1.35)` and `invPow(2.4, 2)`. It\'s a key observation. Now I am trying to understand the mathematical underpinnings that lead to these similarities. The intent is to identify a common thread in their behavior. Next, I\'ll compare the smoothness and behavior of `smPow` and `invPow` further.\n\n\n**Refining Parameter Interactions**\n\nI\'m thrilled with how the new formula integrates seamlessly with existing configurations, avoiding any breaking changes! Examining the `responseCurve` slider\'s effect on `smPow` at various settings (1.0, 1.35) offers key insights. Specifically, the observed finer micro-aiming at lower response values is exciting, particularly at `r=0.15` and `r=0.25` which are much improved. The center precision is also much improved, by over 3x! This is a good direction to go in.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "tDz_aqmNCoKez7IPuoWSsQc"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_6275082814358499587"
      name: "run_command"
      arguments_json: "{\"CommandLine\":\"node -e \'\\nconst dz = 0.06;\\nfunction newCurve(r, p=1.35, k=0.85) {\\n  if (r <= dz) return 0; if (r >= 1) return 1;\\n  const u = (r - dz) / (1 - dz);\\n  const t = Math.pow(u, p);\\n  const s = t * t * (3 - 2 * t);\\n  return s * (k + (1 - k) * s);\\n}\\nfor (const p of [1.0, 1.35, 1.8, 2.4]) {\\n  const vals = [0.25, 0.50, 0.75, 0.90, 1.00].map(r => `${r}:${newCurve(r, p).toFixed(4)}`).join(\\\"  \\\");\\n  console.log(`p=${p.toFixed(2)}: ${vals}`);\\n}\\n\'\", \"Cwd\":\"/app/applet\", \"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":5000, \"toolAction\":\"Checking slider range response\", \"toolSummary\":\"Check slider range response\"}"
